\section{Introduction}
The board has a broadside directional coupler on it.  This paper talks
about the practical aspects of using a directional coupler like this
from a semi-theoretical point of view.  It doesn't go into the deep
theory of how the devices work, see S.B. Cohn's 1960 paper
"Characteristic Impedances of Broadside-Coupled Strip Transmission
Lines" (IRE Trans. MTT) for that sort of detail.

There's a good paper at:
https://markimicrowave.com/assets/e01dd483-b079-4da5-9d03-79b6db1f5969/directivity\_and\_vswr\_measurements.pdf
describing the problems associated with directional couplers.  Though
it doesn't go into the theory as much as you might like.

\section{What Is a Directional Coupler}
A directional coupler is a 4-port device, it has an ``input'' and
``output'' port for the measured signal and coupled ports for the
measurement of forward and reverse power.  These coupled ports measure
the signal; the forward power port will output a signal based on the
power flowing from ``input'' to ``output'' and the reverse power port
will output a signal based on the power flowing the other way.  Thus
``input'' and ``output'' are not good names, since power flows both
ways, but good names are hard to come by.

The amount of power coupled depends on the coupler design and is
generally expressed in dB from the amount of input power to the
coupled power.  So, for instance, if you input 30dBm and the coupled
power was 10dBm, the coupling coefficient would be -20dB.

Each port has an impedance, and it's important to get these right.  As
usual, these are generally designed to be $50\Omega$.

You can buy directional coupler parts, and you can build one using
stripline on a PCB.  It's reasonable to get 20dB of directivity with a
stripline coupler.  For higher directivity you really need a precision
part.

\section{Errors in Measurement}
A directional coupler, like all real devices, has imperfections.  The
basic problem is that some power flowing in the forward direction will
come out the reverse power port, and vice versa.  This is expressed in
directivity, the amount of power flowing out one coupled port that
came from the other direction.  So if the directivity is 20dB, the
amount of power from one direction flowing ``wrong'' port will be 20dB
less than the power flowing in the ``right'' port.

How bad this depends on various imperfections in the layout and
components.

The coupling coefficient can be off, too, but that's not generally as
big an issue.  And it can be compensated for with calibration.

You cannot realistically compensate for directivity error by
calibration with a simple magnitude-only measurement device.  To
compensate by calibration you would need a device that also measure
phase (like a VNA) because the phase of the opposite direction is not
known and it can interfere constructively or destructively.  So if the
directivity is 20dB and the forward power is 30dBm and the reverse
power is 10dBm, the power out the reverse power port will be half from
the forward power (20dB directivity, -30dB coupling, so -20dBm) and
half from the reverse power (-30dB coupling from a 10dBM signal).  If
the signals were in phase, you would measure four times the power,
-14dBm (these are coherent signal, summing them in phase doubles the
voltage, thus quadrupling the power).  If they were 180 degrees out of
phase you would measure no power.  It could be anywhere in-between.

\subsection{Layout Variations}

A directional coupler should be as symmetric as possible to avoid
these sorts of errors.  Even if the layout is perfect, PCB tolerance
can cause asymmetry.  Trace width and layer-to-layer distance, for
instance.  This causes directivity issues, where power from one
direction leaks into the other direction.  One big thing is
layer-to-layer registration, or the alignment error between the
layers.  This can be 75um for standard process, and with a trace
that's 470um wide that's a pretty big variance.  You might be able to
get this down to 35um with a higher precision process.

The impedances on the ports don't seem to be affected so much by this
sort of error.  Some, of course, but it's not as significant as the
directivity.

These errors are difficult to quantify.

\subsection{Coupled Load Mismatch}
The problem with measuring both sides of a directional coupler has to
do with load mismatch on the coupled ends.  If the port is $50\Omega$,
and the load is not, you will get reflections that will, of course,
flow right into the other coupled port.

For precision measurement, one side of a directional coupler is often
terminated with a precision $50\Omega$ resistor.  That way you know
you are not getting significant reflection on that side.  Obviously,
if you want to measure both directions you will need two directional
couplers.  Some manufacturers make devices that have all this built
in.

\section{How Important Are These Errors?}
The importance of all this depends on what you are trying to do.  If
you are trying to build a precision device that can measure return
loss down to 25dB, you are going to need better precision than
standard PCB board manufacturing can do and probably two separate
couplers.  You are going to need very good directivity, on the order
of 40dB.

If you are trying to keep your PA from getting blown and know if you
have a reasonable working antenna on the other end, then precision is
not so important.  One nice thing for this purpose is the error goes
down as the reflected power goes up.  With the previous example of 20
dB of directivity, measuring 10dB of return loss, though not extremely
accurate, is a reasonable thing to do.  Let's use the PacSat board as
an example.

One important thing to note is the forward power measurement is not
going to be inaccurate unless the reflected power is very high.

\section{Example of the PacSat Board}
The PacSat board has a broadside directional coupler with -30dB of
coupling.  The PA is capable of 33dBm (P1dB) output and can withstand
a VSWR of 10:1, so that corresponds to a return loss of 1.75dB, or
around 31.25dBm flowing back into the PA.  Of course you don't want to
get anywhere near that.  Loss one direction through the filter and
matches is around 1dB.  So even at full power, the power reflected
back from the antenna cannot be more than 31dBm.

But as stated, we don't want to get near that.  Assuming the
directivity of the directional coupler is 20dB and we want to keep the
reverse power to less than 27dBm (return loss of 5dB), lets figure out
what we need to do.

If the power flowing forward is 32dB, that means the forward power
into the reverse coupled port will be -18dBm.  Forward power into the
forward port is 2dBm, of course.  With a reflected power of 27dBm, you
would get -3dBm from the reflected power into the reverse coupled
port.  -3dBm is 500uW, -18dBm is 16uW.  If the forward and reverse
signal are opposite phase that would be 337uW, -4.7dBm.  This is a
32\% error.  If they combine in phase that would be 695uW, about a
39\% error.

So -4.7dBm is where you would want to start protecting the PA.  An in
phase combination that would result in -4.7dBm is 205uW, -6.9dBm from
the reverse power, so 23dBm of reverse power, a VWSR of 2:1. So a VSWR
of somewhere between 2 and 3.5 would trigger the protection circuits,
depending on phase.

So it looks quite reasonable to be able to know if the antenna is
deployed and working, and to be able to protect the PA.

